<%#
  The mailer layout, in MJML. Replaces the Inky/foundation_emails layout this
  file used to be; every mailer picks it up through ApplicationMailer's
  `layout "mailer"`, Devise::Mailer included.

  MRML (Rust) compiles it, so the production image needs no Node - which matters
  because the final Docker stage deletes node_modules and app/frontend.

  mrml-rails runs ERB first and compiles only the render that carries the <mjml>
  root, which is this file. The content templates and the partials under
  shared/mailer are therefore plain MJML fragments, and a fragment rendered
  outside this layout would reach the recipient as literal <mj-text> - MRML
  passes tags it does not recognise straight through without an error, so
  MjmlRenderingTest asserts that none survive.
%>
<mjml>
  <mj-head>
    <mj-title><%= I18n.t("title.default") %></mj-title>

    <%#
      No mj-font, deliberately.

      mj-font emits <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/...">, and
      premailer-rails resolves every stylesheet link it finds - through its
      NetworkLoader for a remote one. That put a blocking third-party HTTP
      request on the path of every single delivery, and a Net::OpenTimeout when
      the host was unreachable. The Inky layout never did this: vite_stylesheet_tag
      gave premailer a local path.

      The cost of leaving it out is small. Gmail, Outlook and Yahoo strip
      @font-face regardless, so Orbitron only ever reached Apple Mail. The one
      place it has to be right - the wordmark - is a PNG now, built by
      bin/build-mail-logo. What is left asking for it in CSS is the small
      tracked uppercase label, where the tracking and the uppercase carry the
      treatment on their own. Body copy asks for Open Sans and falls back to
      Helvetica with no visible change in weight or measure.

      If a webfont is ever wanted back, it has to come with a way to keep
      premailer off it - premailer honours data-premailer="ignore" on a link, but
      mj-font emits no such attribute.
    %>
    <mj-attributes>
      <mj-all font-family="<%= MailerTheme::BODY_FONT %>" />
      <mj-body background-color="<%= MailerTheme::BACKGROUND %>" />
      <mj-text color="<%= MailerTheme::TEXT %>" font-size="16px" line-height="1.5" padding="0 0 16px" />
      <mj-divider padding="0" />

      <%#
        typography.scss: h2 is 1.7rem at weight 500, line-height 1.2. There is no
        h1 class: the only h1 a mail had was the wordmark, which is part of the
        header image now.

        No h4 class. The app's h4 is $font-size-base - the same 16px as body copy
        - which works on a page where a card's frame and position already say
        "this is a heading". In a mail it does not: a 16px/500 headline directly
        above 16px/400 body read as two paragraphs, with no hierarchy at all.
        The mails that used <h4> use h2 now, like every other mail.
      %>
      <mj-class name="h2" font-size="27px" font-weight="500" line-height="1.2" color="<%= MailerTheme::TEXT %>" />

      <%# The Orbitron label: small, tracked, uppercase. %>
      <mj-class name="label"
                font-family="<%= MailerTheme::LABEL_FONT %>"
                font-size="12px"
                letter-spacing="2px"
                text-transform="uppercase"
                color="<%= MailerTheme::MUTED %>" />

      <mj-class name="footer" font-size="13px" color="<%= MailerTheme::MUTED %>" line-height="1.6" />
    </mj-attributes>

    <%#
      Keep prose out of the mj-style block below. MRML parses its contents
      looking for tags, so a single angle bracket in a CSS comment - "the / size
      shorthand" written with brackets round the word - aborts the whole compile
      with "unexpected token in root template" and a byte offset. Comments
      without brackets are fine, but ERB comments never reach the parser at all.

      .mail-bg paints the backdrop, and it has to be a stylesheet rule rather
      than mj-wrapper's background-url attributes. Those attributes do work in
      MJML - MRML emits the picture as a shorthand, and generates the VML that
      Outlook reads - but premailer then reduces the shorthand to
      "background: no-repeat center top / cover": css_parser cannot read the
      slash-size form, so it drops the url and the colour with it. Worse, that
      wrecked shorthand then wins the merge against any longhand from a class,
      which is why declaring both routes at once left the mail with no picture
      at all. Longhands on their own come through the pass untouched.

      The cost of giving up the attributes is Outlook, which needs the VML and
      now falls back to background-color. That is the flat black the mail had
      before, so the floor has not moved.
    %>
    <mj-style>
      /*
        The inward-rounded end of each cap - the top cap rounds its bottom
        corners and the bottom cap its top, so the outward edge stays a line
        continuous with the border. Outlook drops border-radius and Gmail strips
        this block in a forwarded message, and both degrade to a square-ended
        bar: the motif survives, the 3px corner does not. Nothing load-bearing
        lives in here for that reason.
      */
      .cap-top p { border-radius: 0 0 <%= MailerTheme::CAP_RADIUS %> <%= MailerTheme::CAP_RADIUS %> !important; }
      .cap-bottom p { border-radius: <%= MailerTheme::CAP_RADIUS %> <%= MailerTheme::CAP_RADIUS %> 0 0 !important; }

      /*
        Hover, on the button. Btn lights the end-cap and lifts the surface and
        the label, and leaves the frame alone for every tone but the two that
        flood - a recoloured border reads as the control changing shape. These
        rules restate that, and only that.

        They have to live here rather than inline because no style attribute can
        carry a pseudo-class, and they need !important because the resting value
        they replace IS an inline style. premailer keeps a selector containing
        :hover out of its inlining pass and re-emits it into a style block, so
        the rules survive the delivery hook - but Gmail drops this block in a
        forwarded message and Outlook's Word engine has no hover at all, which
        is the same floor the cap radius above accepts.
      */
      .mail-btn:hover { background-color: <%= MailerTheme::CONTROL_HOVER %> !important; }
      .mail-btn:hover .mail-btn__label { color: <%= MailerTheme::LIFTED %> !important; }

      /*
        Neutral borrows the primary cap while hovered, which is the treatment
        the :primary tone already wears at rest - so a primary button lifts its
        surface and its label and keeps the cap it had.
      */
      .mail-btn--neutral:hover .mail-btn__cap { background-color: <%= mail_accent %> !important; }

      .mail-btn--danger:hover {
        background-color: <%= MailerTheme::DANGER %> !important;
        border-color: <%= MailerTheme::DANGER %> !important;
      }
      .mail-btn--danger:hover .mail-btn__label { color: <%= MailerTheme::FLOOD_TEXT %> !important; }
      .mail-btn--danger:hover .mail-btn__cap { background-color: <%= MailerTheme::DANGER_CAP_HOVER %> !important; }

      .mail-btn--warning:hover {
        background-color: <%= MailerTheme::WARNING %> !important;
        border-color: <%= MailerTheme::WARNING %> !important;
      }
      .mail-btn--warning:hover .mail-btn__label { color: <%= MailerTheme::FLOOD_TEXT %> !important; }
      .mail-btn--warning:hover .mail-btn__cap { background-color: <%= MailerTheme::WARNING_CAP_HOVER %> !important; }

      .mail-panel > table {
        border: 2px solid <%= MailerTheme::EDGE %> !important;
        border-radius: <%= MailerTheme::RADIUS_SURFACE %> !important;
        border-collapse: separate !important;
      }

      .mail-bg {
        background-image: url('<%= vite_asset_url("images/mail-bg.jpg") %>') !important;
        background-position: center top !important;
        background-repeat: no-repeat !important;
        background-size: cover !important;
        background-color: <%= MailerTheme::BACKGROUND %> !important;
      }
    </mj-style>
  </mj-head>

  <mj-body background-color="<%= MailerTheme::BACKGROUND %>" width="<%= MailerTheme::WIDTH %>">

    <%#
      The app's backdrop. BackgroundImage renders bg-6 fixed and cover at
      opacity .4 over black; email has no opacity to give, so
      images/mail-bg.jpg is that composite baked into the file.

      Dimmed to .30 rather than the app's .40, and the difference is not taste.
      On the page the backdrop only ever sits behind panels, so page text never
      touches it. In a mail the title, both transmission labels and the whole
      footer sit directly on the picture, and bg-6's brightest region - the
      planet limb - runs straight across the top where the title goes. Measured
      against that peak, #c8c8c8 scores 3.05:1 at .40 and 4.90:1 at .30, so .30
      is the first step that clears AA for the small labels. Tune this one
      number if the picture wants to be more or less present.

      Regenerate with:
        magick app/frontend/images/bg-6.jpg -resize 1200x -evaluate multiply 0.30 \
          -strip -interlace Plane -quality 72 app/frontend/images/mail-bg.jpg

      31KB, because 30% of a night sky compresses to almost nothing. It is a
      remote image, so it costs the message nothing and only loads if the reader
      allows images - which is also why it can never be load-bearing:
      background-color carries the mail on its own, and that is the flat black
      the mail had before.

      full-width so the picture reaches the window edges while the content stays
      at 640px, which is how the app shows it.
    %>
    <mj-wrapper full-width="full-width"
                background-url="<%= vite_asset_url("images/mail-bg.jpg") %>"
                background-size="cover"
                background-repeat="no-repeat"
                background-position="center top"
                background-color="<%= MailerTheme::BACKGROUND %>"
                css-class="mail-bg"
                padding="0">

    <%#
      The header lockup - the planet mark over the wordmark - is one image
      because the wordmark is set in Orbitron, and Gmail, Outlook and Yahoo all
      strip @font-face. As live text it fell back to Helvetica in exactly the
      clients most people read mail in, which is the one place the app's display
      face has to be right. Baked into a PNG, every client shows it.

      Generated by bin/build-mail-logo, which typesets it in Chromium from the
      same woff2 the app serves. Rendered at 2x and displayed at 285px, so it
      stays sharp on a retina screen; rerun that script if the width changes.

      alt is the wordmark itself, so a reader with images off still gets the
      name - and it is the only place it appears now, so nothing repeats it. The
      old layout paired a decorative icon with an <h1> saying the same thing,
      which premailer read out twice in the text part.
    %>
    <mj-section padding="32px 0 4px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-image src="<%= vite_asset_url("images/mail-logo.png") %>"
                  alt="<%= I18n.t("title.default") %>"
                  width="285px"
                  padding="0" />
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <mj-section padding="12px 0 20px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-text mj-class="label" align="center" padding="0">
          <%= I18n.t("mailer.transmission_start") %>
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <%#
      The panel: --color-surface fill, a 2px --color-edge frame, --radius-surface.

      The frame comes from .mail-panel in mj-style rather than mj-wrapper's own
      `border` attribute, because that attribute lands on the inner td while the
      radius and the fill land on the table around it. Two things then go wrong
      at once: premailer drops the td's border-radius, and even with it there a
      cell's radius is inert under border-collapse: collapse, which is what MJML
      sets. The result was a rounded fill inside a square frame.

      Putting all three on the table - with border-collapse: separate, which is
      what lets a radius clip a border at all - keeps them on one element.
      cellspacing="0" is already on the tag, so `separate` adds no gaps.

      The table is deliberately the host rather than the wrapper's outer div,
      which also carries the fill and the radius: Outlook ignores divs but does
      render this table, so it degrades to a square 2px frame instead of losing
      the frame entirely.
    %>
    <mj-wrapper background-color="<%= MailerTheme::SURFACE %>"
                border-radius="<%= MailerTheme::RADIUS_SURFACE %>"
                css-class="mail-panel"
                padding="0">
      <%= render "shared/mailer/cap", side: "top" %>

      <%#
        Bottom padding is 24 rather than 12 so that with the 4px every template
        leaves on its last element the frame breathes equally top and bottom. At
        12 the bottom cap crowded the final line while the top had 28px of air,
        which on a two-line notification was the whole panel.
      %>
      <mj-section padding="28px 28px 24px" background-color="transparent">
        <mj-column padding="0">
          <%= yield %>
        </mj-column>
      </mj-section>

      <%= render "shared/mailer/cap", side: "bottom" %>
    </mj-wrapper>

    <mj-section padding="24px 0 6px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-text mj-class="label" align="center" padding="0">
          <%= I18n.t("mailer.transmission_end") %>
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <mj-section padding="10px 0 32px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-text mj-class="footer" align="center" padding="0">
          <%= I18n.t("copyright") %> &copy; <%= Time.current.year %>
          <%= Rails.configuration.app.copyright_owner %>
          <br />
          <a href="<%= frontend_privacy_policy_url %>" style="color: <%= mail_accent %>;"><%= I18n.t("mailer.privacy") %></a>
          &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp;
          <a href="<%= frontend_settings_notifications_url %>" style="color: <%= mail_accent %>;"><%= I18n.t("mailer.notification_settings") %></a>
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    </mj-wrapper>

  </mj-body>
</mjml>
